---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/CaseStudySection.astro";

const threads = [
  {
    key: "search",
    label: "Search",
    owned: "Built the Next.js search experience, including ranking, filters, and result views, then retired Elasticsearch in favour of PostgreSQL.",
    enables: "People can find relevant policies without a separate search index that must be kept in sync with the source records."
  },
  {
    key: "docs",
    label: "Documentation",
    owned: "Built Markdown parsing and React rendering, connected to Directus publishing.",
    enables: "Content teams can maintain help and API guidance without editing the page implementation."
  },
  {
    key: "data",
    label: "Data quality",
    owned: "Led the data team and built a feedback loop with data stewards for missing and malformed records.",
    enables: "The people maintaining the source records can investigate gaps exposed by search and product use."
  }
];
const mechanisms = [
  {
    thread: "search",
    layer: "Match the search architecture to the scale",
    detail: "We used Elasticsearch historically for fuzzy matching and ranking. PostgreSQL met the product's actual scale, so we retired Elasticsearch and its synchronisation layer. The search still needs to handle imperfect queries and useful filters."
  },
  {
    thread: "docs",
    layer: "Separate content from its rendering",
    detail: "Markdown is parsed into an intermediate structure and rendered in React. The publishing connection lets editors change the guidance while the application owns how it appears."
  },
  {
    thread: "data",
    layer: "Check the records behind the interface",
    detail: "Record checks and feedback to data stewards sit alongside search work. Improving a query cannot supply a field that is missing from the source."
  }
];
const initialThread = "search";
---

<CaseStudyLayout title="Open Policy Finder" description="Leading data and search work for Jisc's publishing-policy service, including the decision to retire Elasticsearch." threads={threads} initialThread={initialThread}>
  <p slot="intro">Researchers need to find the publishing policy relevant to their journal or publisher. In Jisc's Sherpa Team, I built the product's search experience and led data work behind the records people depend on.</p>
  <dl slot="meta" class="case-meta" aria-label="Case study summary">
    <div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Jisc · Sherpa Team / April 2024–February 2026</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Focus</dt><dd>Next.js, PostgreSQL search, documentation, data quality</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Project</dt><dd><a href="https://openpolicyfinder.jisc.ac.uk">openpolicyfinder.jisc.ac.uk</a></dd></div>
  </dl>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-problem" title="Problem">
    <p>People search with partial titles, acronyms, publisher names, and imperfect recall. A relevant result also depends on the source records being complete enough to use, while the guidance needs to stay editable by the content team.</p>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-contribution" title="My contribution">
    <table class="case-ledger">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">What I built and why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{threads.map((thread) => (
        <tr data-thread={thread.key} data-active={thread.key === initialThread ? true : undefined}>
          <th scope="row">{thread.label}</th><td><p>{thread.owned}</p><p>{thread.enables}</p></td>
        </tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-decisions" title="Decisions">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <table class="case-ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Choice</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{mechanisms.map((mechanism) => (
          <tr data-thread={mechanism.thread} data-active={mechanism.thread === initialThread ? true : undefined}>
            <th scope="row">{mechanism.layer}</th><td>{mechanism.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}</tbody>
      </table>

    </div>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-result" title="What it enables">
    <div class="case-prose"><p>The work connected policy discovery with the content and data workflows behind it. Retiring Elasticsearch removed a second copy of the search data and the work of keeping it aligned with PostgreSQL. Separately, the data-steward feedback loop made source-record problems visible to the people who could address them.</p><p>I also started local engineering-harness and MCP work in the Sherpa Team. It could inspect the codebase, query data, run checks, and interact with the authenticated application. That was early tooling, rather than a claim of team-wide adoption.</p></div>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-lesson" title="Lesson">
    <p>Search quality crosses team boundaries. A confusing result might need a query change, clearer guidance, or a correction to the source record. Working through those possibilities with the people who own the data is part of building the search.</p>
  </CaseStudySection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
